It is entirely possible to build a shiny app out of components from the shiny package itself. However, the “modern” shiny design focuses on a different package called “bslib” which provides access to the highly flexible bootstrap framework. Bslib allows a number of UI components with a high degree of customization, and which renders nicely, even on a small screen such as a phone.
Bslib features both pages (used to define the overall rules for our app page) and the components of the pages (especially as units of UI elements called “cards”). We can take our previous code and modify it to become a navbar page.

app &lt;- shiny::shinyApp(
  ui = bslib::page_sidebar(
    title = "Our Iris plot",
    sidebar = "here is our sidebar",
    shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot"
    )
  ),
  server = function(input, output){
    output$iris_plot &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(
      iris_plot
    )
  }
)
shiny::runApp(app)

If we paste this code into our console and runs it (rerunning the code for the iris plot if necessary), we will see we now have a sidebar where we can put something in. Let’s leave it empty for now, and instead focus on the other functionality of bslib we teased before - the cards.
Cards are an effective way to organize plots and other UI components. They are easy to implement - we simply wrap our UI content (in this case the Iris plot) in bslib::card()

app &lt;- shiny::shinyApp(
  ui = bslib::page_sidebar(
    title = "Our Iris plot",
    sidebar = "here is our sidebar",
    bslib::c(shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot")
    )
  ),
  server = function(input, output){
    output$iris_plot &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(
      iris_plot
    )
  }
)
shiny::runApp(app)

In itself this does not do much. However, cards have some attributes such as headers that can be modified if we for example want to give our plot a header.

app &lt;- shiny::shinyApp(
  ui = bslib::page_sidebar(
    title = "Our Iris plot",
    sidebar = "here is our sidebar",
    bslib::card(
      bslib::card_header("A beautiful plot about flowers"),
      shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot")
    )
  ),
  server = function(input, output){
    output$iris_plot &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(
      iris_plot
    )
  }
)
shiny::runApp(app)

The true power of cards becomes evident once we start having several of them. Let’s make a few more plots so we can see how bslib behaves once we have multiple:

iris_plot_2 &lt;- ggplot2::ggplot(iris,
                               ggplot2::aes(x = Sepal.Length, y = Sepal.Width, color = Species)) +
  ggplot2::geom_point()


iris_plot_3 &lt;- ggplot2::ggplot(iris,
                               ggplot2::aes(x = Petal.Length, y = Petal.Width, color = Species)) +
  ggplot2::geom_point()

