Lets add our two new plots to our layout, copy it to the console, and run it:

app &lt;- shiny::shinyApp(
  ui = bslib::page_sidebar(
    title = "Our Iris plot",
    sidebar = "here is our sidebar",
    bslib::card(
      bslib::card_header("A beautiful plot about flowers"),
      shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot")
    ),
    bslib::card(
      bslib::card_header("Another beautiful plot about flowers"),
      shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot_2")
    ),
    bslib::card(
      bslib::card_header("OKay I think I've had enough flower plots for now"),
      shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot_3")
    )
  ),
  server = function(input, output){
    output$iris_plot &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(
      iris_plot
    )
    output$iris_plot_2 &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(
      iris_plot_2
    )
    output$iris_plot_3 &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(
      iris_plot_3
    )
  }
)
shiny::runApp(app)

It looks nice! if we want to change the order of our cards, we can add another layer from bslib, and define how many columns we want. We wrap all our cards in a function called bslib::layout_column_wrap() and add the argument width  = 1/2 to get 2 columns (if this is indeed what we want). If we instead had written width = 1/3 we would get 3 columns and so forth.

app &lt;- shiny::shinyApp(
  ui = bslib::page_sidebar(
    title = "Our Iris plot",
    sidebar = "here is our sidebar",
    bslib::layout_column_wrap(
      width = 1/2,
      bslib::card(
        bslib::card_header("A beautiful plot about flowers"),
        shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot")
      ),
      bslib::card(
        bslib::card_header("A beautiful plot about flowers"),
        shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot_2")
      ),
      bslib::card(
        bslib::card_header("A beautiful plot about flowers"),
        shiny::plotOutput(outputId = "iris_plot_3")
      )
    )
  ),
  server = function(input, output) {
    output$iris_plot &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(iris_plot)
    output$iris_plot_2 &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(iris_plot_2)
    output$iris_plot_3 &lt;- shiny::renderPlot(iris_plot_3)
  }
)
shiny::runApp(app)

There is much more functionality to building a good user interface and there are many resources on bslib. But for now we know how to build a Shiny interface
